# Claude Code brief — Color naming + palette matching (Color Finder)

## Goal
When a user uploads/snaps a photo in the Color Finder (on the Daily Inspo page,
coco-daily-inspo.cultureschool.org), take the extracted hex and show:
(a) the closest human color NAME from the xkcd dataset, and
(b) the closest 3 palettes from our own `palettes` library.
All client-side. No third-party color APIs. No Pantone or paint-brand data.

## Before coding — audit
The Color Finder already extracts a hex from an uploaded image. Find that
extraction point and build on it. Do NOT rebuild extraction.

## Data
- xkcd colors: build `xkcd-colors-lab.json` from the source rgb.txt at
 https://xkcd.com/color/rgb.txt. Parse name + hex, precompute
  CIELAB for each, write the JSON, ship as a static asset loaded once.
  DO NOT hand-enter color values — build from the real file.
- Our palettes: query the existing `palettes` table. Match against
  [ DECIDE: `dominant_color` (simplest, column exists) OR per-swatch across all colors ].

## Matching
- hex → CIELAB conversion.
- Distance = Delta-E. Use CIE76 to start; leave a clear seam to swap in
  CIEDE2000 later.
- Precompute Lab for BOTH reference sets at load, not per-comparison.
- Two lookups on the same extracted hex: nearest 1 xkcd name, nearest 3 palettes.

## UI
In the Color Finder result, show the extracted swatch, its nearest xkcd name,
and the 3 nearest palettes (link each palette the way the app already does).

## Constraints
- Client-side only.
- Never load proprietary color libraries (Pantone, paint brands) into the DB.
- Match must run in Lab space, never raw RGB.
- No unverified cultural-origin claims attached to color names.

## Deliverables
1. Build script + generated xkcd-colors-lab.json.
2. Matching module (hex→Lab, Delta-E, nearest()).
3. Color Finder UI wired in.
4. Quick test with 3–4 sample hexes showing name + palette matches.

